# Deploy & environments (Vercel + Next.js + Firebase)

## Environments
- Support two environments: local development and production (Vercel).
- Never commit secrets to git. `.env.local` must be in `.gitignore`.
- All required environment variables must be validated in `src/lib/env.ts` with clear error messages.

## Required env vars (Firebase client)
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID

## Optional env vars (future integrations)
- MERCADOPAGO_ACCESS_TOKEN (server-only)
- MERCADOPAGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET (server-only)
- GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_ID (server-only)
- GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_SECRET (server-only)
- GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REDIRECT_URL (server-only)
- GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY (can be public but must be restricted by referrers)

Rules:
- Any server-only env var must NOT be prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
- Client code must never read server-only env vars.
- If a feature requires server-only secrets, implement it via Next.js Route Handlers / API routes (or Cloud Functions), never in client components.

## Deployment target (recommended)
- Primary hosting: Vercel.
- Use Vercel project settings to add env vars for Production and Preview.
- After adding/changing env vars, trigger a redeploy.

## Build correctness
- Before declaring a feature done, ensure:
  - `npm run build` passes locally
  - `npm run lint` passes (if configured)
- Avoid relying on dev-only behavior. Assume production is stricter.

## URLs & Auth configuration
- Firebase Auth must allow the deployed domain (Vercel URL/custom domain) under Authorized domains.
- For Google Sign-In, ensure OAuth config supports:
  - localhost (dev)
  - production domain (Vercel)
- App routing should not depend on absolute localhost URLs.

## API routes / webhooks (payments/calendar)
- Webhooks must be implemented server-side only (Route Handlers / API routes).
- Any webhook endpoint must:
  - verify signature/secret (when available)
  - validate payload
  - be idempotent (safe to receive duplicates)
  - write to Firestore with minimal permissions (admin SDK later) or strict rules

## Firestore Rules & Production safety
- Never deploy to production with permissive Firestore rules (e.g., `allow read, write: if true`).
- If rules are not ready for a feature, the UI must be gated/disabled for non-admin.

## README requirements (must be kept updated)
README.md must include:
- Local setup steps
- Required env vars list
- How to run dev server
- How to build for production
- How to deploy to Vercel (high level)
- Troubleshooting section for common env/auth domain issues

## Logging & debugging
- Avoid noisy console logs in production.
- Prefer structured error messages in services; UI should show user-friendly messages.
